  <h1>Mabi</h1>
  
 </div>
 <div class="story-body" id="content">
  <p>Mabi nodded. She looked back at the water. The northeast gut was running fast, the current pulling toward the open ocean, and she could feel — could actually feel, the way you feel a hand on your shoulder — the moment when the current passed over the deep rock formation, the moment when the water hit the place where Mabi turned, and the way the current split and reformed on the other side like something that had been broken and put back together by someone who remembered what it was supposed to be.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>She felt it as clearly as she felt her own heartbeat.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>She did not tell Old Pauley. He had said he didn't ask questions, and she did not want to answer them.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>She was seventeen when she went down to the water for the first time alone.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>This was not forbidden — she had swum alone since she was twelve, since her grandmother decided she was old enough to make the choices that swimming alone required — but it was different now. She was going down not to swim but to call. She had known this was coming for months, had felt it building in her the way pressure builds before a storm, and she had resisted it the way you resist a thing that you know is necessary but that you are not ready for, and then she had stopped resisting, and then she had come down to the water at three in the morning when the island was sleeping and the tide was high and the moon was full.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>The beach she chose was Small Coral, the one on the western side of the island where the sand was white and the water was clear and the reef was close enough that she could see the fish moving beneath the surface like thoughts moving through water. She took off her shoes. She rolled up her pants. She walked into the water until it was at her waist and then she stood there, in the current, and said the name.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>"Mabi," she said. Her voice was quiet. The ocean did not care about volume. This was something her grandmother had taught her — that the old things do not need to be shouted, that they are not deaf but rather are attenuated to frequencies that volume cannot reach, that calling is not a conference of volume but a matter of attention, of willingness to stand in the presence of something that has been present all along.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>The water was cold. This was not unusual for three in the morning, and the current was smooth, running north along the island's western coast toward the place where the island ended and the open ocean began. She felt it against her legs, her hips, her waist, the particular pressure of moving water against a body standing in it.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>She said the name again. "Mabi."</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>The current stopped.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>Not slowed. Stopped. The water around her — within a radius she could not measure, in a circle whose edges she could not define — went from moving to still, and the stillness was absolute, like the stillness of a held breath, like the stillness of a moment that is about to become something else.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>Mabi stood in the still water and felt the first cold thread of something that was not fear but was adjacent to fear, was the thing that fear grows from, the thing that lives in the basement of the self where the true animal lives, the self that knows what it is and does not forget it even when the waking mind would prefer to.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>She did not move. She did not speak. She waited.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>The water moved again. But it moved differently now — not as a current moving in a direction but as a presence moving through the current, as something that was not the water but that was using the water the way a body uses blood, flowing through channels that were also the body's channels and that were also something else, something older, something that had been flowing through the rocks and the soil and the deep places of the island long before there were people to see it.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>She felt Mabi arrive the way you feel a storm arriving from a great distance, first as a pressure change, then as a color change in the water, then as a shape that was not a shape, a form that revealed itself for a moment in thepatterns of the current and then retreated again into the water and the dark.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>Mabi was not a figure. Mabi was not a face or a body or any of the things that stories make the old things into. Mabi was a quality of attention, a way the water had of looking at her, a shift in the density of the medium that surrounded her and that was also, in some essential way, Mabi's body — the body she had no choice but to wear in this world, the body that was the island's water and the island's wells and the rain that fell on the rocks and the soil and the roots of the plants that grew in the island's thin, sandy soil.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>Hello, child, Mabi said.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>Mabi did not speak with a voice. Mabi spoke the way deep water speaks, which is to say through pressure and temperature and the movement of molecules, through the language of chemistry and physics that the body understands before the mind does, the language that Mabi's grandmother had known and that her grandmother's grandmother had known and that had been passing down through the women of the island since before there were words for what was being passed.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>Mabi understood this. She did not hear the words but she received them, the way a vessel receives water — completely, without effort, the water going where the vessel is shaped to hold it.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>Hello, Mabi said. You've grown.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>Mabi had not seen Mabi since she was eleven, since her grandmother had sat with her on the porch and told her about the name, and in the years since she had sometimes wondered if she had imagined it, if the name was just a name, just a story that old women tell children on porches, just the way island people explain things they don't understand. But she knew now, standing in the still water under the full moon, that she had not imagined it. Mabi was real. Mabi had been there all along. Mabi had been waiting for her to call.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>Why did you need to call? Mabi asked. Your grandmother did not call until she was thirty.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>I don't know, Mabi said. The truth, delivered the way truth must be delivered to something that cannot be deceived, which is to say simply, without defense, the way a riverbed is simply the shape the water has made by flowing.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>Mabi was quiet. The water around her was quiet. The whole island was quiet, the whole night was quiet, and Mabi existed in the quiet the way she had always existed, in the deep places, in the silence between the waves.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>Your grandmother is gone, Mabi said. This was not a question.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>Yes.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>And you stayed.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>Yes.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>Mabi considered this. Mabi existed in the water and the water was all around the island, all through the island, and Mabi had been all through the island for so long that Mabi had been there when the island was born, when the volcano that was the island's core had pushed up through the sea and begun the long process of becoming rock and soil and the shape of land. Mabi knew things about the island that no human knew, that no human had ever known, and Mabi stored these things the way the water stores them — without preference, without agenda, the way a library stores books it has not yet been asked to produce.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>You have a choice, Mabi said. This is not a thing we usually offer. Your grandmother did not have a choice. Her grandmother did not have a choice. The gift goes where it goes, and the ones who receive it carry it whether they want to or not. But you are different. You called on your own. You came to the water on your own. That changes things. That means you are not just a vessel. It means you are a citizen, of a kind. And citizens can choose.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>Mabi did not know what the choice was.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
